    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>On a hotel claim, the physical damage and the lost room revenue are tied together. A water loss on one floor can take dozens of rooms out of inventory, and each day of slow decision-making on the repair scope adds to the business income claim. Owners also face questions that other properties don't: brand standards, franchise improvement plans, and the speed at which mold develops in occupied guest rooms. We handle the building claim and the income claim as one problem.</p>

<h2>Room revenue and business income</h2>
<p>Hotel business income claims are usually built from daily operating data. Useful records include:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Property management system reports showing rooms available, rooms sold, and average daily rate</li>
<li>Monthly profit and loss statements for at least the prior year, plus the same months the year before</li>
<li>STR or similar market reports, if you subscribe, to show what nearby hotels did during the same period</li>
<li>A list of out-of-order rooms by date</li>
<li>Group bookings and events that were canceled because of the damage</li>
</ul>
<p>Seasonality matters. A loss that closes rooms during a peak period costs more than one in a slow month, and the claim should reflect that. Insurers sometimes argue that rooms out of service wouldn't have sold anyway; market data and booking history are how you answer that. The coverage itself, including any waiting period and the length of the period of restoration, is set by your business income form.</p>

<h2>Guest displacement</h2>
<p>When rooms become unusable with guests in the house, you may relocate guests to other properties, refund stays, or comp nights. Costs you incur to reduce the loss, such as walking guests to a nearby hotel, may be claimable as extra expense, depending on the policy. Keep the folio records and receipts for each relocated guest. Guests' own belongings are a separate matter, usually handled under their own insurance or your liability coverage, not your property claim.</p>

<h2>FF&amp;E: furniture, fixtures, and equipment</h2>
<p>A guest room has a predictable list of furniture, fixtures, and equipment: beds, case goods, soft seating, drapery, carpet, TVs, artwork, lamps, safes, and mini-fridges. Carriers often price these at generic retail values or apply heavy depreciation. Hotel FF&amp;E is commercial-grade and often specified by the brand, so it costs more than residential equivalents. We build the contents part of the claim room type by room type, using your purchase records or the brand's specification where available.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Mold risk after water losses</h2>
<p>Hotels combine many bathrooms, constant air conditioning, and vinyl wall coverings that trap moisture behind them. After a leak, mold can grow behind vinyl wall coverings and in wall cavities without showing on the surface. Fast drying and moisture mapping matter for guest health and for the claim, because many commercial policies limit or exclude mold, often with a sublimit for mold that results from a covered water loss. We take moisture readings behind wall coverings, not only on the surface, and keep the mitigation company's daily logs as part of the record. Our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page covers that process in more detail.</p>

<h2>Brand standards and PIP requirements</h2>
<p>Franchised hotels must meet brand standards, and repaired rooms often have to match the current brand specification. That raises a fair question about what the insurer owes.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Usually part of the claim</h3><ul><li>Repairing or replacing damaged items with materials of like kind and quality</li><li>Matching repaired areas to undamaged areas, where the policy wording supports it</li><li>Reasonable costs to meet brand specifications for the items that were damaged</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>Usually not part of the claim</h3><ul><li>Property improvement plan (PIP) work the brand required before the loss</li><li>Upgrades to undamaged rooms to a new brand standard</li><li>Changes required by a new franchise agreement or re-flag</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>If you were already planning PIP work, the two projects can sometimes be done together, but the costs must be separated. We keep the claim limited to the damage and its covered consequences, which makes it harder for the carrier to dismiss the whole estimate as an upgrade.</p>

<h2>Hotels hit by Texas storms</h2>
<p>Coastal hotels in Galveston, Corpus Christi, and the Rio Grande Valley deal with hurricane wind, wind-driven rain, and in some cases TWIA policies with their own one-year filing deadline. Hotels along the interstates in Central and North Texas see more hail. For coastal losses, see <a href="/services/hurricane-windstorm-claims/">hurricane and windstorm claims</a>. A multi-building motel with a blanket limit raises the same per-building deductible questions covered in our <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims overview</a>.</p>
